          <h3>Our Congo Greys, Ready to Reserve</h3>
          <p>{congoBirds.length} single Congos and one companion pair carry a Congo badge above. <StatementLabel kind="fact" /> A Congo is the larger of the two greys we breed, {congoWeight} grams as an adult, with the bright red tail most people picture when they hear the species name. Bery is the grown hen, Amie the youngest, Roys the one who will meet your visitors at the door of his cage.</p>
          <h4>How Each Bird Is Priced</h4>
          <p>The card price is the whole price of the bird. It is not a starting figure, it is not before paperwork, and it does not change once you have paid a deposit. What moves it between one Congo and another is what the next heading says.</p>
          <h5>Sex, Age and Documentation Set the Number</h5>
          <p>A weaned chick from this season sits higher than a grown hen who has been with us a year, because the chick carries the months of hand-feeding that a buyer pays for once and keeps for the life of the bird. Sex is on the laboratory certificate rather than in our judgement, and the price does not favour one over the other. The documentation is the same folder for every bird, so it never explains a difference between two cards.</p>
          <h6>Inventory Note: Which Birds Ship and Which Are Pickup Only</h6>
          <p>Every weaned bird on this page can fly. A bird still finishing its hand-feedings, which today means Amie, stays with us until it eats on its own, whichever state it is going to. The companion pair travels best to the door rather than through a cargo desk, and we say so on the pair's card.</p>

          <h3>Our Timneh Greys, Ready to Reserve</h3>
          <p>Elad and Evie are the two Timnehs, a cock and a hen from different clutches. A Timneh is the smaller grey, {timnehWeight} grams, with a maroon tail rather than a red one and a horn-coloured patch on the upper beak. In our nursery the Timnehs are the ones that step onto a stranger's hand first.</p>
          <h4>Why a Timneh Lists Below a Congo</h4>
          <p>Demand, not quality. More buyers arrive asking for the red tail, so the Congo band runs higher. The Timneh band, {timnehRange}, is where our floor sits, and a buyer who wants a calmer, quicker-to-settle grey is usually better served by it than by paying more for the name.</p>
          <h5>Same Screening, Same Paperwork, Smaller Bird</h5>
          <p>The laboratory panel, the sex certificate and the treaty file do not know the difference between the two subspecies. A Timneh leaves with the same six documents a Congo does, in the same folder, with the same written guarantee.</p>

          <h3>Reserved Birds and the Waiting List</h3>
          <p>When a deposit lands on a bird, its card comes off this page rather than gaining a badge, because a bird that is spoken for is not for sale. If the grey you wanted is gone by the time you read this, the form at the bottom takes your name for the next clutch, and we write to that list before anything is listed here.</p>
