          <h3>Size and Colouring at a Glance</h3>
          <p>Put a Timneh next to a Congo and it reads as roughly a third smaller: darker over the back, maroon in the tail, pale on the upper beak. The Congo is paler, heavier, and carries the red tail. Both are Psittacus erithacus, one species with two recognised forms, and both talk; the size difference is the one that changes a cage, a perch and a travel crate.</p>

          <h3>Temperament Differences We See in the Nursery</h3>
          <p>We hand-feed both in the same kitchen, on the same schedule, and the difference shows up before either is weaned. Timneh chicks accept a new hand sooner. Congo chicks pick their person and take longer to admit a second one. Neither is a rule, but across the clutches we have raised it has held often enough to say out loud.</p>
          <h4>Which Subspecies Settles Faster With Children</h4>
          <p>The Timneh, in our experience, because a child's movements are quick and a Timneh forgives quick. A Congo in a house with young children does well when one adult is clearly the bird's person and the children are taught to let the bird come to them.</p>
          <h5>The Midland Socialization Method Applies to Both</h5>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="method" /> The Midland Socialization Method is our name for how a chick is handled between weaning and leaving: family handling by more than one person, an out-of-cage routine every day, and exposure to the ordinary noise of a household. Both subspecies go through it, and it is why a bird from us steps onto a hand rather than backing away from one.</p>
          <h6>Across the Cluster: Our Congo vs Timneh Page Goes Deeper</h6>
          <p>Voice, lifespan, the cost of a cage for each, and the mistakes buyers make when they choose by colour alone are on <a href="/congo-vs-timneh-african-grey/">the full Congo versus Timneh comparison</a>. This page gives you enough to choose a card; that one gives you enough to defend the choice.</p>

          <h3>The Price Gap Between the Two</h3>
          <p>Today the gap is the distance between {timnehRange} and {congoRange}. It reflects demand for the red tail, not a difference in what leaves with the bird. A Timneh at {floor} arrives with the same laboratory results and the same paperwork as a Congo at {ceiling} does.</p>
