          <h3>Why Our Floor Is $1,500 and Not $500</h3>
          <p>Because {floor} is the least a screened, documented Timneh costs us to raise and still leave with its file complete. Behind the number sit three to four months of hand-feeding, a laboratory panel, a sex test, a veterinary visit and the treaty paperwork, none of which gets cheaper because a listing wants a lower figure. Below that, one of them has been skipped, and it is never the one the seller tells you about.</p>
          <h4>What the $500 Classified Leaves Out</h4>
          <p>Usually the bird. The pattern we hear about from buyers who call us afterwards is a deposit paid to a listing with no address, then a shipping fee, then an insurance fee, then silence. When a real bird does exist at that price it is an unweaned chick sold early, or a grey with no screening and no paperwork, which means no way to know what it is carrying and no lawful way to move it across a border.</p>
          <h5>PCR Disease Screening and DNA Sexing Are Inside the Price</h5>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="fact" /> The laboratory panel runs before a bird is listed, and the sex certificate comes from the same laboratory. Neither is an add-on. A buyer who asks a $500 seller for either usually finds that the conversation ends.</p>
          <h6>Before You Reserve: What a Deposit Holds and for How Long</h6>
          <p>{deposit} takes one bird off this page for the time you need to book a flight or a drive, and we deduct it from the balance when you collect. It is not open-ended; if plans change, tell us and we will talk about it rather than quietly relisting the bird.</p>

          <h3>What a $200 Deposit Does</h3>
          <p>It moves a bird from this page to your name. What it does not do is buy a photograph or a promise: before any deposit we set up a video call so you have watched the bird move, in our house, on the day you pay.</p>

          <h3>Shipping Is Its Own Line</h3>
          <p>None of the prices above include travel, because travel depends on where you are and a buyer within driving distance should not pay for a flight. The two flat figures below are the whole of it for a single bird anywhere in the country.</p>
          <h4>Airport Pickup at $185 or Home Delivery at $350</h4>
