          <h2>How Your African Grey Travels: Flight, Nanny or Midland Pickup</h2>
          <p class="opener">A grey leaves us one of three ways, and all three start the same: a health certificate, a booked date, and a bird that is fully weaned. The strip below is the whole journey in three steps; the headings after it are the detail.</p>

          <ol class="tstrip" aria-label="The three steps from our aviary to you">
            <li><span class="ts-n">1</span><span class="ts-txt"><b>The certificate is issued</b><span>An avian vet examines the bird and signs the travel certificate</span></span></li>
            <li><span class="ts-n">2</span><span class="ts-txt"><b>We book the flight</b><span>Cargo counter at {airport}, your door at {home}, or a nanny's cabin seat</span></span></li>
            <li><span class="ts-n">3</span><span class="ts-txt"><b>Handover and the first 72 hours</b><span>You collect the bird; the written guarantee clock starts</span></span></li>
          </ol>

          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "how-an-african-grey-gets-home-map-hub-760.webp"} srcset={IMG + "how-an-african-grey-gets-home-map-hub-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "how-an-african-grey-gets-home-map-hub.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Map of the United States showing the route from deposit and vet clearance to travel crate and arrival">

          <h3>Step One: The Health Certificate Is Issued</h3>
          <p>Before any bird flies, an avian veterinarian examines it and issues the certificate the airline requires. It travels fastened to the crate, not in an envelope, because the cargo counter at each end checks it. A bird that is not weaned does not get one from us, whatever the buyer's schedule.</p>

          <h3>Step Two: We Book the Flight</h3>
          <p>We book, we crate, we drive the bird to the airport. You choose the destination: the cargo counter at your nearest major airport, your front door, or a seat in the cabin with a flight nanny who hands the bird to you at arrivals.</p>
          <h4>Which Cargo Programs We Use</h4>
          <p>Delta Cargo, United Cargo and American Airlines Cargo all move live animals under the same set of rules, and we pick by the shortest day for the bird rather than by the cheapest fare. A direct flight beats a connection every time, even when the connection lands closer to you.</p>
          <h5>IATA Live Animals Regulations Set the Crate and the Route</h5>
          <p>The crate size, the ventilation, the water cup and the temperature window a bird may fly in are all set by the International Air Transport Association's live animal rules, which every carrier we use follows. In the hottest weeks of a west Texas summer that can mean an early departure or a delayed one; we tell you which before you plan a day around it.</p>
          <h6>Inventory Note: Pickup Within the Midland Radius Skips the Flight</h6>
          <p>A buyer within a two to three hour drive of us collects in person, meets the bird before it is boxed, and pays nothing for the journey. That radius is not a service area we advertise; it is how far a weaned grey rides comfortably on a lap.</p>

          <h3>Step Three: Handover and the First 72 Hours</h3>
          <p>The journey ends when the bird is in your hands, at a counter or a doorstep or our kitchen. That moment matters for more than the photograph, because it is when the guarantee begins.</p>
          <h4>The Guarantee Clock Starts at Handover</h4>
          <p>Our written health guarantee runs 72 hours from possession, and anything transit-related is covered for the first 24. A same-day call is what makes either usable, because it lets us arrange an avian vet near you while the window is open. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-health-guarantee/">The guarantee page</a> has the term word for word.</p>
