          <h3>CITES Appendix I Paperwork for a Captive-Bred Bird</h3>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="fact" /> The African Grey sits on the strictest listing of the international treaty on trade in endangered species. What that governs is commercial trade in wild-caught birds. A grey hatched in our aviary is captive-bred, and the paperwork that says so is the document that lets it be sold, moved and owned lawfully.</p>
          <h4>Why Appendix I Does Not Stop You Owning One</h4>
          <p>Because the listing is about where a bird came from, not who may keep it. A grey hatched here with its file in order is as lawful to own as a budgie. What the listing does stop is the trade in birds trapped in the wild, which is the trade every cheap classified without paperwork is, knowingly or not, a part of.</p>
          <h5>The Hatch Certificate and Closed Band Tie the Bird to Our Aviary</h5>
          <p>The ring goes onto a chick's leg in its first days, when the foot is small enough for it to pass; it cannot be fitted to a grown bird. Its number is on the certificate with the date and the parents, so the bird in your hand and the paper in your folder can be matched by anyone, for the rest of its life.</p>
          <h6>Across the Cluster: The Documentation Page Explains Each Form</h6>
          <p>Every document in the register above has a longer entry on <a href="/cites-african-grey-documentation/">the documentation page</a>, with what a real one looks like and the questions to ask a seller who cannot produce it.</p>

          <h3>Screening Results, Not Promises</h3>
          <p>"Vet checked" is a phrase; a laboratory result is a document. Every bird here has the second, and we hand it over rather than describe it.</p>
          <h4>PBFD, Polyomavirus and Psittacosis by PCR</h4>
          <p>Those are the three diseases a buyer of a young grey should fear most, and all three are screened by a method that looks for the organism itself rather than for symptoms that may not have appeared yet. The results are in the folder with the bird's name on them.</p>
          <h5>A DNA Sex Certificate for Every Single Bird</h5>
          <p>Cock and hen greys look alike, so the sex on the card is the sex on the certificate, from a feather sample run by the same laboratory. We sex every single bird before it is listed; the companion pair is offered on the strength of how the two birds get along, which no certificate measures.</p>

          <h3>Our USDA Licence Is Searchable</h3>
          <p>We hold a licence under the Animal Welfare Act, and it is the one credential a buyer can check without trusting us at all.</p>
          <h4>How to Look Us Up on APHIS</h4>
          <p>The Department of Agriculture's inspection service publishes a public search of licence holders. Search it for us before you send a deposit to anyone, including us. A seller who is not in it has no licence to lose.</p>
