          <h2>Who Raises These African Greys, and Where?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Two people, one house in west Texas, and a nursery that has been running since 2014. There is no shop front and no staff; if you call, one of us answers.</p>

          <div class="split">
            <figure class="split-image">
              <img src={IMG + "two-greys-midland-texas-sign-hub-760.webp"} srcset={IMG + "two-greys-midland-texas-sign-hub-480.webp 480w, " + IMG + "two-greys-midland-texas-sign-hub-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "two-greys-midland-texas-sign-hub.webp 1040w"} sizes="(max-width:1024px) 100vw, 440px" width="1040" height="780" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Two young African Greys beside a handwritten sign reading Midland, Texas">
              <figcaption>Two of ours, photographed at home with the sign a buyer asked us to hold up</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="split-text">
              <h3>Mark and Teri Benjamin, Midland, Texas, Since 2014</h3>
              <p>We are Mark and Teri Benjamin. We keep a small number of breeding pairs, hand-feed every chick they produce in our kitchen, and place each one with a household we have spoken to. That is the whole business.</p>
              <ul class="split-list">
                <li><span set:html={tick} />One aviary, one address, one licence you can search</li>
                <li><span set:html={tick} />Every chick hand-fed by the two of us</li>
                <li><span set:html={tick} />Video call before any deposit, no exceptions</li>
                <li><span set:html={tick} />Pickup in person inside a two to three hour drive</li>
              </ul>
              <h4>The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol in One Paragraph</h4>
              <p><StatementLabel kind="method" /> The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol is our name for how a chick gets from the nest box to a buyer: pulled for hand-feeding at the age we have found works, fed on a schedule in the house rather than a shed, weaned onto pellets and fresh food, and not listed until it eats on its own.</p>
              <h5>Hand-Feeding and Weaning to the Sixteen-Week Gate</h5>
              <p>Weaning finishes somewhere between twelve and sixteen weeks, and the gate is the day the chick eats enough on its own that a missed hand-feeding does not matter. No grey leaves before it, whatever a buyer's calendar says.</p>
              <h6>Before You Reserve: Questions We Answer on the First Call</h6>
              <p>Which parents, which clutch, what the bird eats today, what it weighed this morning, and which of the two of us it prefers. Ask; we would rather answer than have you guess.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3>The Midland Socialization Method</h3>
          <p>Defined once above, and worth its own heading here because it is the part of raising a grey that a laboratory cannot certify. A chick that has only ever been fed and left alone grows into a bird that bites. A chick handled by several people, carried from room to room and let out every day grows into the bird on the cards above.</p>
          <h4>Family Handling and the Out-of-Cage Routine</h4>
          <p>From weaning until the day it leaves, every bird here is handled by both of us and by whoever is in the house, and has time out of the cage every day. It is a habit, not a program, and it is why our birds arrive already expecting to be part of a family rather than kept apart from one.</p>
          <div class="ticket"><a class="ticket-cta" href="#reserve">Book a call and meet a bird <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" decoding="async"></div>

        <!-- ===================== §7 · NEXT · T3 BOARDING PASS, REFRESHED TO A SPOKE INDEX ===================== -->
